<!-- Nakshatras Tab -->
<div id="nakshatras" class="tab-content py-10 px-5">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto">
    <div class="mb-12 reveal">
      <span class="section-eyebrow">SECTION 04</span>
      <h2 class="section-title mt-2">NAKSHATRAS — THE LUNAR MANSIONS</h2>
      <p class="section-sub mt-3">
        The 27 (traditionally 28 with Abhijit) Nakshatras are lunar constellations spanning 13°20' each. They form the most ancient layer of Vedic astrology, deeply tied to the Moon's rhythm and the soul's subtle desires.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      id="nakshatraGrid"
      class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4 stagger"
    ></div>
  </div>

  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto mt-16 reveal">
    <h3
      class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold mb-3"
      style="font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--gold2)"
    >
      PADA — THE QUARTERS OF A NAKSHATRA
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm md:text-base mb-6 max-w-4xl" style="color: var(--muted)">
      Each Nakshatra is divided into four equal parts called
      <strong>Padas</strong>
      , meaning "quarters" or "feet." This gives every Nakshatra a finer layer of meaning: the Nakshatra shows the broader lunar archetype, while the Pada reveals the more specific style, direction, and expression of that archetype.
    </p>
    <div
      class="rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8"
      style="background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--border)"
    >
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
            1. BASIC STRUCTURE
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--cream)">
            One Nakshatra spans
            <span class="readout">13°20'</span>
            . It is divided into
            <span class="readout">4 Padas</span>
            , each spanning
            <span class="readout">3°20'</span>
            . Across 27 Nakshatras, this produces
            <span class="readout">108 Padas</span>
            in total.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
            2. WHY IT MATTERS
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--cream)">
            The Pada adds precision. Two planets in the same Nakshatra can behave differently if they fall in different Padas. This is why experienced Jyotish readers often go beyond the Nakshatra name and look at the Pada.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
            3. NAVAMSA LINK
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--cream)">
            Every Pada maps directly to one Navamsa division. This makes the Pada a bridge between the
            <strong>Nakshatra</strong>
            and the
            <strong>D9 / Navamsa chart</strong>
            , which is why it is so important in deeper interpretation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 stagger">
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
            PADA 1
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold mb-2" style="font-family: var(--font-display)">
            Dharma
          </h4>
          <p class="text-sm" style="color: var(--muted)">
            Purpose, direction, initiation, self-expression, and the urge to act correctly.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
            PADA 2
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold mb-2" style="font-family: var(--font-display)">
            Artha
          </h4>
          <p class="text-sm" style="color: var(--muted)">
            Resources, stability, usefulness, material support, and practical building.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
            PADA 3
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold mb-2" style="font-family: var(--font-display)">
            Kama
          </h4>
          <p class="text-sm" style="color: var(--muted)">
            Desire, relationship, curiosity, pleasure, communication, and social expression.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
            PADA 4
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold mb-2" style="font-family: var(--font-display)">
            Moksha
          </h4>
          <p class="text-sm" style="color: var(--muted)">
            Depth, release, inwardness, spiritualization, and the urge to transcend.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-6 info-box">
        <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
          HOW TO READ A PADA
        </div>
        <p
          class="text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed"
          style="color: var(--cream)"
        >
          Think of the Nakshatra as the main archetype and the Pada as its expression style. For example, a planet in the same Nakshatra can feel more fiery, earthy, airy, or watery depending on which Pada it occupies. In practice, this makes the reading much more precise: the Nakshatra tells
          <em>what</em>
          the energy is, while the Pada tells
          <em>how</em>
          it manifests.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
            PRACTICAL USES
          </div>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-2" style="color: var(--muted)">
            <li>• Refines planetary interpretation inside a Nakshatra</li>
            <li>• Helps explain why same-Nakshatra placements differ</li>
            <li>• Supports Navamsa (D9) understanding</li>
            <li>• Used in naming syllables and traditional calculations</li>
            <li>• Adds depth to personality and predictive analysis</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="data-card">
          <div class="section-eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem">
            SIMPLE MEMORY LINE
          </div>
          <p
            class="text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed"
            style="color: var(--cream)"
          >
            <strong>Nakshatra = the story.</strong>
            <br />
            <strong>Pada = the chapter of that story.</strong>
            <br />
            <strong>Navamsa = the deeper meaning behind it.</strong>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
